---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<h1>Data Structures</h1>
			<p>
				A series working through Robert Sedgewick's
				<a href="https://www.coursera.org/learn/algorithms-part1">Algorithms, Part I</a> in modern C++,
				one data structure at a time.
			</p>
			<p>
				The rule for every post is the same: derive the cost on paper, then measure it, and publish
				both. When the model and the stopwatch disagree — and once cache behaviour is involved they
				often do — that disagreement is the interesting part.
			</p>
			<p>
				Every post ships with the code it talks about, in the same commit, in the
				<a href="https://github.com/khalidHsoliman/data-structures">series repo</a>. Clone it, build
				it in release, and check the numbers against your own hardware.
			</p>
			<p>
				Start with <a href="/blog/">the posts</a>, or subscribe by <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>.
			</p>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
